<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/shingles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/shingles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/shingles.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/appraisal-work/">Appraisal work</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Roof &amp; Hail Claim Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal work</p><h1>Roof and Hail Claim Appraisal in Maine</h1><p class="lede">Hail is less common in Maine than in the Plains, but summer storms do bruise roofs in western and interior towns. A roof hail claim appraisal in Maine sorts out the real question: repair a few squares, or replace a roof that can no longer be matched?</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="spec"><div><dt>Common in</dt><dd>June–August storms</dd></div><div><dt>Typical issue</dt><dd>Repair vs. replace</dd></div><div><dt>Our role</dt><dd>Your named appraiser</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Hail and roof losses in Maine</h2>
<p>Maine’s hail tends to fall in summer thunderstorms across Oxford, Franklin, Androscoggin and Kennebec counties, with occasional events in the interior of York and Penobscot. Stones are usually small, but repeated impacts can fracture asphalt mat, dent soft metal, split old cedar and crack slate. Roofs also take damage from wind, falling limbs and snow load, which is why a roof hail claim appraisal in Maine often overlaps with other perils.</p>

<h2>What roof estimates commonly miss</h2>
<p>Disputes on roof claims tend to cluster around a handful of issues:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Repair versus replacement.</strong> Carriers often price a spot repair on a slope that cannot be repaired without breaking surrounding shingles or slate.</li>
<li><strong>Matching.</strong> Discontinued shingle colors and weathered cedar rarely blend with new material on the same slope.</li>
<li><strong>Measurements.</strong> Pitch, waste and squares calculated from an aerial image rather than the actual roof. Our <a href="/claim-math/roof-pitch-squares-converter/">roof pitch and squares converter</a> helps you check the math.</li>
<li><strong>Underlayment and code items.</strong> Ice and water shield at eaves and valleys, drip edge and ventilation required when a roof is replaced.</li>
<li><strong>Steep and high work.</strong> Steep-pitch charges, staging for three-story farmhouses and safety equipment for slate and metal roofs.</li>
<li><strong>Metal roofs.</strong> Standing-seam panels with functional damage at seams versus cosmetic dents, which some policies treat differently.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Documenting a roof claim</h2>
<p>Hail evidence fades. Take dated photos of soft metals such as vents, gutters and downspouts, window screens, siding and the roof surface itself if you can do so safely. Keep any hail you collect in a freezer with a coin for scale. Save the weather service storm report for your town and the date. Ask your roofer to mark and photograph test squares. If your policy has a separate wind or hail deductible stated as a percentage, check it with our <a href="/claim-math/percent-deductible-checker/">percent deductible checker</a>.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Cosmetic damage exclusions</b><p>Some policies exclude cosmetic damage to metal roofs, and some limit roof coverage to actual cash value based on age. Those are coverage terms, not pricing questions. Read your endorsements, and talk to a Maine attorney if the carrier relies on one to deny or limit the claim.</p></div>

<h2>How appraisal applies to roofs</h2>
<p>When the carrier accepts that hail or wind damaged the roof but disputes how much, appraisal decides the amount, which often includes whether the damage requires replacement to restore the roof. We measure the roof ourselves, document damage per slope, price materials available in Maine, and present the file to the carrier’s appraiser. If the two appraisers do not agree, the umpire reviews the differences.</p>

<h3>Illustrative example</h3>
<p>Illustrative example: after a July storm in Farmington, the carrier pays $4,200 to repair two slopes of a 26-square architectural shingle roof. The shingle line is discontinued and the replacement product does not match. The owner’s estimate prices full replacement with ice and water shield and steep charges at $23,800. The gap turns on scope, which appraisal can resolve. Figures are invented for illustration.</p>

<h2>When to look elsewhere</h2>
<p>If the insurer says there was no storm damage at all, blames wear and tear, or denies the claim on a policy exclusion, that is a coverage dispute. An attorney is the right resource. Appraisal works when the carrier agrees there is covered roof damage and the argument is about how much it costs to fix.</p>
<h2>Questions about roof &amp; hail claim appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does Maine get enough hail for a roof claim?</summary><p>Hail is less frequent than in some regions, but summer storms do damage roofs in western and interior Maine. A dated storm report and photos of soft metal damage help show it happened.</p></details><details><summary>Can a roof hail claim appraisal in Maine require a full replacement?</summary><p>Appraisal sets the amount of loss, and in many files that amount reflects the cost to replace a roof that cannot be repaired or matched. Coverage limits in your policy still apply.</p></details><details><summary>What about slate and cedar roofs?</summary><p>Both need specialized labor and materials. We price them as they are, not as asphalt shingles, which is a common source of underpayment.</p></details><details><summary>My policy has a separate wind and hail deductible. Does that change appraisal?</summary><p>The deductible is applied after the amount of loss is set. A percentage deductible can be large, so check the dollar figure before deciding whether appraisal is worth it.</p></details></div>
